                "text": "I'm trying to figure out the selection rule by comparing cell counts per shape across examples \u2014 looking for whether the smallest or unique cell count determines which shape gets picked, but the pattern isn't holding consistently since ties appear in example 3.\n\nI'm now checking whether uniqueness comes from shape congruence instead of size \u2014 testing if two shapes are just rotations of each other while a third is genuinely distinct, and cross-referencing that against which shape was actually chosen as the answer, along with position-based explanations like top-right or top-middle placement.\n\nCounting cells doesn't distinguish uniquely either \u2014 ex3's shape 1 has a different cell count but that's not the answer. Maybe symmetry is the key: ex1's 6-shape looks symmetric, while ex2's 7-shape looks asymmetric, so I should check whether the target shape is the odd one out in terms of symmetry rather than count or congruence.\n\nLet me test bounding-box area instead: computing width\u00d7height for each shape per example to see if the smallest (or largest) box consistently picks the answer shape.\n\nChecking example 2 gives a tie between two shapes with area 8, resolved by fewer cells favoring one of them. Example 3 gives a three-way tie, so I try checking whether some shapes are just rotations of each other by comparing their normalized cell coordinates.\n\nRotating shape 4 by 90 degrees clockwise produces exactly shape 8's cell pattern, confirming they're congruent, while shape 3 (an S-tetromino) isn't congruent to either, and shape 1 has 7 cells. Yet the answer is 8, which is part of the congruent pair rather than the unique one, so my congruence theory doesn't hold \u2014 maybe the rule instead concerns concavity, hollowness, or positional preference like picking the topmost shape.\n\nNone of position-based rules (topmost, leftmost) or symmetry-based rules cleanly distinguish shape 8 across examples 1-3, so I'm going back to compare example 2's shapes more carefully, starting with mapping out the 7-shape's cell coordinates to identify its pentomino type.\n\nCell counts don't cleanly explain it either\u2014ex1 gives 6,8,8,8 picking the odd one out, but ex2's 5,10,8,7 breaks that pattern. Checking reading order for tiebreaks among ex3's tied shapes doesn't point to 8 either, so maybe the rule involves structural differences like holes or notches, or congruence under reflection rather than raw cell count.\n\nTesting whether shapes are true reflections versus just rotations of each other doesn't resolve it cleanly, so I'm considering other angles\u2014maybe it's about which shape touches the grid edge. In ex1 the shape touches the top edge, and in ex2 as well, so that might be the actual distinguishing feature.\n\nTrying color-based reasoning next: checking if the chosen shape always has the maximum color value doesn't hold consistently across examples, since ex2's answer isn't the max. I'm now considering bounding box area minus cell count as a way to measure \"holes\" in each shape, comparing that across examples to see if it picks out the odd one.\n\nTesting the ratio of filled cells to bounding box area, which works for ex1 but not ex2, so that's not the rule either. I'm now wondering if the real pattern is that three of the four shapes are congruent to each other and one is the outlier, so I'm mapping out the exact cell coordinates of each shape to check for congruence.\n\nThese shapes turn out to have different bounding-box dimensions, so congruence doesn't hold. I'm rechecking the minimum-cell-count idea, which fits ex1 and ex2, but ex3 has a tie among several 4-cell shapes yet the answer is a different one, so I suspect I'm miscounting the cells for some shapes.\n\nAfter recounting, shapes 3, 4, and 8 all have exactly 4 cells, creating a tie I can't break with cell count alone. I'm exploring other tiebreakers\u2014uniqueness of shape under rotation/reflection, or some symmetry proper